    <div class="foot-method">
      <h3>About this assessment &amp; methodology</h3>
      <p>This assessment reflects Merkle's analytics consulting practice &mdash; a structured, vendor-neutral way to match analytics needs to the platforms in market, refined across enterprise platform selections. It is not a feature checklist: every judgement in the underlying rubric encodes our consultants' hands-on experience of where each platform genuinely performs or falls short in production.</p>
      <p>The method is deliberately simple and defensible. Capabilities are grouped into pillars. You assign a MoSCoW priority to each capability &mdash; <b>Must</b>, <b>Should</b>, <b>Could</b> or <b>Won't</b> &mdash; to express how important it is. Each platform's coverage of a capability is measured from a yes/no assessment of its sub-capabilities, maintained in our master rubric, and rolls up to a priority-weighted fit score (Must&nbsp;3, Should&nbsp;2, Could&nbsp;1). A Must a platform cannot meet at all is treated as a disqualifier rather than a deduction, so a strong all-rounder never outranks a platform that clears every non-negotiable. The output is a best-of-breed recommendation: a lead platform, plus any specialist platforms that close priority gaps.</p>
      <p class="foot-fine">Assessments represent Merkle's point of view and are validated against current vendor releases at the time of engagement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>
